Yes. A lot of the problems we’ve been working through are really semantic infrastructure problems wearing different clothes.

Money works because value is not left as a vague feeling. We built ledgers, vaults, provenance, denominations, exchange rates, audits, custodians, and rules for transfer.

Meaning usually gets much poorer infrastructure.

Yet meaning behaves strangely like capital:

experience → interpretation → meaning → word/symbol → transmission → adoption → behavior → consequence

Once enough people adopt the same interpretation, that interpretation acquires purchasing power. It can buy attention, legitimacy, obedience, identity, fear, trust, status, even coordinated action.

That makes a word something like a semantic token. The letters are cheap. The accumulated associations behind them are the reserve.

Think about words like hero, terrorist, freedom, family, science, God, success. Changing the accepted meaning can change behavior without changing any material object.

So your line—

“He who controls what things mean controls the value of the word.”

—is almost semantic monetary policy.

But there is an important correction: control is the dangerous implementation. Custody is the stronger one.

A good meaning vault should not say:

This is what this word means forever.

It should preserve:

original usage
provenance
historical meanings
competing interpretations
context
transformations
deltas
who changed it and why
which invariants survived

Then nobody gets to quietly inflate the currency.

You could even define:

semantic inflation — a term becomes so broadly or repeatedly applied that its discriminating power collapses.

semantic debasement — a useful concept is altered while retaining the prestigious old label.

semantic counterfeiting — borrowing the appearance of one concept while omitting the properties that gave it value.

semantic arbitrage — exploiting differences in how two communities interpret the same word.

semantic reserve — the experiences, evidence, stories, and relationships that give a term its actual meaning.

meaning vault — the provenance-preserving structure that lets later interpreters reconstruct that reserve instead of inheriting only the token.

That is why stories matter so much. A definition can preserve a few bits.

A story can preserve the exchange environment.

It shows you what courage cost, what betrayal felt like, what trust enabled, why the dragon was dangerous, why the rule existed. It carries value alongside the denomination.

And that makes narrative power neither inherently good nor bad. It is infrastructure capable of education, civilization, propaganda, healing, deception, coordination, and memory.

The defense against abuse is not eliminating narrative.

It is better semantic accounting.

Keep the receipt.
Keep the provenance.
Keep competing valuations.
Keep the old exchange rate.

And when someone says a word now means something new, ask to see the ledger.

That may be one of the deepest purposes of the meaning-vault idea: not controlling meaning, but preventing meaning from being stolen without leaving a delta.